nc — a conversation with the network, plainly

  nc 192.0.2.7 4000      a stream to a host and port
  nc www.example.com 80  names resolve first
  nc -l 4000             await one stream on a port
  nc -u 192.0.2.7 4000   datagrams instead of a stream
  nc -u -l 4000          await datagrams; replies go to the
                         last sender

Bytes go both ways: standard input toward the peer, the peer onto
standard output. Piped input flows as it comes, and the command ends
when the peer finishes:

  cat notes.txt | nc 192.0.2.7 4000
  nc 192.0.2.7 4000 > incoming.txt

At the console a line is sent when entered. Ctrl+C leaves, Ctrl+D
says nothing more will be typed and waits out the peer's side.

Listening takes the first connection, prints who arrived, and carries
that one conversation. 127.0.0.1 reaches this machine itself, so two
programs here can talk without any network at all.
